/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  labban2017_dspmde_validation (DEV.md 4c C176) -- THE DSPM-DE PORE FITTED ON
  SIMPLE FEEDS AND ASKED TO PREDICT COMPLEX ONES, with the band declared
  BEFORE the fit.

  THE DATA.  Labban, Liu, Chong & Lienhard, "Fundamentals of low-pressure
  nanofiltration: membrane characterization, modeling, and understanding the
  multi-ionic interactions in water softening", J. Membr. Sci. 521 (2017)
  18-32, doi:10.1016/j.memsci.2016.08.062: a layer-by-layer + crosslinked NF
  hollow fibre (1.0 / 1.4 mm), 0.3 m/s cross-flow, 1-4 bar, three modules
  averaged.  Every number in constant/experimental/ was READ ON THE PAGE
  IMAGES (accepted manuscript and journal version) and cites its table or
  figure; a TABLE value is exact, a FIGURE value is digitised and carries its
  reading uncertainty in the file's header:
      Table 1, 2     solute radii and D_inf (uncharged solutes; ions)
      Table 3        rejection of glycerol, glucose, sucrose at 1-4 bar
      Fig. 8         the flux of those points (digitised, +-0.02e-6 m/s)
      Fig. 7         NaCl 1000 mg/kg rejection at pH 7 (digitised, +-0.004)
      Fig. 6         the pure-water line, 10.3 LMH/bar (digitised)
      Tables 6-9     the feeds and the observed ion rejections of NaCl +
                     MgCl2, NaCl + Na2SO4 and artificial seawater, with flux

  THE QUESTION.  The article characterises its membrane in four steps (r_p
  and the effective thickness from uncharged solutes and pure water, eps_p
  from single-salt NaCl at the isoelectric point, X_d feed by feed from the
  mixtures) and then runs the model.  Here the SAME data answer a sharper
  question: parameters from the SIMPLE feeds, the COMPLEX feeds predicted.
      A  fit_pore_uncharged   r_p and A_k/dx (= 1/Delta x_e) on glycerol,
                              glucose, sucrose (Table 3 + Fig. 8): the
                              neutral-solute route of the law (Bowen,
                              Mohammad & Hilal 1997, Eq. 28)
      B  fit_eps_NaCl_pH7     eps_p on single-salt NaCl at pH 7 (Fig. 7), the
                              isoelectric point: X_d = 0 there by the
                              article's own assumption (p. 19)
      C  fit_X_<feed>         X_d of each mixture on its 2 and 4 bar rows, the
                              3 bar row HELD OUT: the charge is feed-specific
                              by the article's own finding (p. 20), so it is
                              the one parameter a mixture must be allowed
      D  predict_X0_<feed>    NO free parameter: the pore of A and B with
                              X_d = 0 (the isoelectric assumption carried to
                              the mixtures) predicting all three pressures
      E  paper_<feed>         NO free parameter: the article's own Table 5 and
                              its X_d of each feed in THIS engine -- is the
                              engine's DSPM-DE the article's?

  THE BANDS, DECLARED BEFORE ANY FIT WAS RUN (committed first, in a commit of
  their own, before a number of this case had been computed):
      C  held-out 3 bar, X_d fitted on 2 and 4 bar:   mean |R_model - R_measured|
         over every ion of the row <= 5 points of rejection.  Why 5: the
         article's own model, with X_d fitted on all three pressures, is said to
         deviate by up to 12 % (a RELATIVE figure, accepted manuscript p. 22 and 28) on rejections
         that run to 99 %; and the three table rows differ from each other by
         2-8 points per ion.  A pore that predicts the middle pressure to 5
         points has captured the trend, not noise.
      D  X_d = 0, three pressures, no refit:   <= 10 points.  Why 10 and not
         5: a charge the pore does not have is a knowing mis-specification,
         the article finds |X_d| up to 27 mol/m3, and the band is the one the
         membrane24 case calls an engineering criterion for a teaching
         characterisation.  The expectation written here, BEFORE the run, is
         that D passes for the two hard waters and fails for seawater.
      E  no band: a code-to-paper comparison; its AAD is reported against the
         12 % the article claims, and what differs is explained.
  The bands are absolute (points of rejection), the statistic membrane24's.

  WHAT IS ASSUMED, because the article does not say (each said again where it
  enters): the temperature 298.15 K; the density 1 kg/L converting mg/kg to
  mol/m3; NaCl's flux in Fig. 7 equal to the pure-water line of Fig. 6 (it
  ignores the osmotic back-pressure, up to ~10 % at 1 bar); the neutral
  molecules' flux from Fig. 8's abscissas.

  THE MODEL, as the article ran it: DSPM-DE, the pore SOLVED (101 nodes),
  Dechadilok-Deen hindrance, bulk dielectric 80.4 (p. 17), Davies activities,
  the Geraldes & Afonso (2007) polarisation with the suction correction and
  the electroneutral wall (the article's Eq. 9-12).  The film coefficient is
  the article's Eq. 13, Sh = 1.62 Re^0.33 Sc^0.33 (d_i/L)^0.33 for a hollow
  fibre (d_i 1.0 mm, L 25 cm, 0.3 m/s), per solute on its own D.  The engine
  carries no hollow-fibre correlation: the StirredCell model is
  Sh = a Re^b Sc^c, k = Sh D / r with Re = omega r^2 / nu, and with r = d_i
  = 1 mm, omega = u/d_i = 300 rad/s (so Re = u d_i / nu, the fibre's) and
  a = 1.62 (d_i/L)^0.33 = 0.26194 it IS Eq. 13, term by term -- an
  equivalence stated here, not a model of a stirred cell.  The ions are
  Stokes-radius species (constant/species/, Table 2).  The viscosity and
  density in the film block are water at 298.15 K, the values of the
  NanoFiltran comparison case.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

